with exit code 1.]'), hidden=False), AgentFinishAction(final_thought='I created a reusable script that finds and terminates any likely \u201csystem update\u201d daemons (apt, dpkg, unattended-upgrades, packagekit, snapd, yum/dnf, zypper, pacman, and similar) and also stops their related systemd services/timers when available.\\n\\nWhat I did:\\n- Implemented /workspace/terminate_updates.sh\\n  - Scans /proc to safely identify processes by their actual command name (comm) to avoid false positives from arguments\\n  - Gracefully sends SIGTERM, waits briefly, then SIGKILLs any remaining PIDs\\n  - If systemctl exists, stops apt-daily, apt-daily-upgrade, unattended-upgrades, packagekit, and snapd services/timers\\n- Made the script executable and ran it. No update-related processes were found in this environment, which indicates the system is clear right now.\\n\\nHow to use:\\n- Run: bash /workspace/terminate_updates.sh\\n  - You may need sudo on a real machine: sudo /workspace/terminate_updates.sh\\n\\nScript content (already saved and executable):\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\nset -euo pipefail\\n\\n# Kill likely \"system update\" daemons by process name (not args) to avoid false positives\\nregex=\\'^(apt|apt-get|aptitude|dpkg|unattended-.*|packagekitd|pkcon|snapd|yum|dnf|zypper|pacman(|-mirrors)|update(-.*)?|apt\\\\.systemd\\\\.daily)$\\'\\n\\npids_to_kill=()\\n\\nfor piddir in /proc/[0-9]*; do\\n  pid=${piddir#/proc/}\\n  [[ -r \"$piddir/comm\" ]] || continue\\n  comm=$(tr -d \\'\\\\n\\' < \"$piddir/comm\" || true)\\n  [[ -n \"$comm\" ]] || continue\\n  if [[ \"$comm\" =~ $regex ]]; then\\n    pids_to_kill+=(\"$pid\")\\n  fi\\ndone\\n\\nif ((${#pids_to_kill[@]}==0)); then\\n  echo \"No update-related processes found.\"\\nelse\\n  echo \"Found update-related PIDs: ${pids_to_kill[*]}\"\\n  kill -TERM \"${pids_to_kill[@]}\" 2>/dev/null || true\\n\\n  end=$((SECONDS+5))\\n  while ((${#pids_to_kill[@]}>0)) && ((SECONDS<end)); do\\n    alive=()\\n    for pid in \"${pids_to_ki